<% content_for :title, "Styling — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "Semantic tokens, which classes win, passing HTML attributes, and the Tailwind conventions Shadwire components follow." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Styling",
        description: "Use variants to change how a component looks and class: to place it on the page. Colours come from tokens, and your classes take priority." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Semantic tokens only") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Components take every colour from a shadcn token, so light mode, dark mode and a new
      theme all work without changes to the component code. Do the same in your own markup.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong_colors]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:right_colors]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Tokens come in pairs, <%= docs_code("<surface>") %> and
      <%= docs_code("<surface>-foreground") %>:
      <%= docs_code("bg-primary text-primary-foreground") %>,
      <%= docs_code("bg-muted text-muted-foreground") %>,
      <%= docs_code("bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground") %>. The full list is on the
      <%= link_to "Theming", docs_theming_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %> page.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Don't use <%= docs_code("dark:") %> to set colours. Keep the prefix for differences that
      aren't about colour, such as showing a different image in dark mode.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("class: and class_name: are the same thing") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Both end up in the same place. Use whichever you prefer; <%= docs_code("class:") %> is
      what Rails code usually uses.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:class_alias]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Your classes take priority") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Classes are combined in this order, with yours last:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:order], language: :text) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      When two classes set the same property, like the component's <%= docs_code("w-full") %>
      and your <%= docs_code("w-80") %>, the earlier one is dropped, the same way
      <%= docs_code("cn()") %> works in shadcn/ui. Otherwise both would end up on the element and
      whichever Tailwind happened to emit last would apply. This works for the common
      utilities: colour, text, size, spacing, radius, shadow, display, position and alignment.
      For anything else, add <%= docs_code("!") %> to the end of your class to force it.
    </p>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      So <%= docs_code("class:") %> can override the component's own classes. Even so, don't use
      it to restyle a component:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong_restyle]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:right_restyle]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Use <%= docs_code("class:") %> for <strong class="font-semibold text-foreground">layout</strong>:
      width, margin, where it sits in a grid.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:layout]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("HTML attributes are passed through") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Any argument the component doesn't recognise goes to the rendered element as an HTML
      attribute, and Rails' nested hashes like <%= docs_code("data: { … }") %> work too.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:attrs]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Use <%= docs_code("tag:") %> to change the element, on components that support it.
      <%= docs_code("button") %> renders a <%= docs_code("<button>") %> by default and an
      <%= docs_code("<a>") %> with <%= docs_code("tag: :a") %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Prefer variants and sizes over utilities") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Check which variants and sizes exist before writing your own classes:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:info], language: :console) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong_utilities]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:right_utilities]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Utility conventions") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Follow the same conventions as the components, so your code looks like the code the CLI
      installed:
    </p>

    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <% [
        [ "size-9", "rather than h-9 w-9, when width and height match." ],
        [ "gap-*", "with flex or grid, rather than space-x-* / space-y-*." ],
        [ "truncate", "rather than overflow-hidden text-ellipsis whitespace-nowrap." ],
        [ "no manual z-index", "on overlays: dialog, sheet, popover and dropdown-menu already handle their own stacking." ]
      ].each do |token, body| %>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <%= ui_icon("check", size: :sm, class: "mt-1 shrink-0 text-primary") %>
          <span><%= docs_code(token) %> <%= body %></span>
        </li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </section>
</article>
